Suicide prevention is a topic that we often don’t think about until we are directly impacted by the loss of a loved one or a member of our community. Even saying the word “suicide” can feel taboo and bring about feelings of shame or discomfort.
As community members we must work to break down the barriers that prevent us from talking about suicide. We must understand how to recognize suicide warning signs, how to talk with a young person who is suicidal, and how to get help.
The Youth Mental Health First Aid (YMHFA) training teaches community members how to help. The 6-hour virtual or in-person course trains adults how to recognize the warning signs of suicide, how to approach and talk to a young person who is in crisis or who is suicidal, and where to go to get professional help.
If you are concerned that a young person is displaying suicide warning signs, it is important to act immediately. Do not leave the young person alone.
In comments marking Suicide Prevention Awareness Month, Vermont Governor Phil Scott joined top state health officials in urging Vermonters to check in with one another and take care of their own mental health, emphasizing the every-day role all of us can play in preventing death by suicide. read more
At the recent Vermont Suicide Prevention Symposium, speaker after speaker drove home one core message: If you notice something, ask the question directly: Are you thinking of killing yourself?
Cecilia Hayes knows what it’s like to come to Vermont from a vastly different culture. Because of her own lived experience, she now helps other Spanish speakers in the Latinx and migrant farm worker communities.
How can we be that adult? How can parents and other adults engender an environment of positive mental and emotional well-being?
These questions were explored by Lauren Biever of Nationwide Children’s Hospital’ Suicide Prevention Center in a recent webinar sponsored by the ROX Institute. Biever sounded a call to action to stem the rise in suicide among young people.
“One of the best things we can do to prevent suicide is to have people talking about it,” Biever said. “We just need to talk to our kids and ask the hard questions.”
Validate their feelings. That doesn’t mean you necessarily agree with them, but it shows that they are being heard.
Accept that, as a parent, you may not be their first go-to for talking about some things. Encourage them to cultivate relationships with other people they can confide in.
