A material is considered biodegradable if it reaches 90% of biodegradation after 6 months. A biodegradable product decomposes and becomes bio-assimilable under the action of micro-organisms, oxygen, temperature, humidity and heat.
Indeed, to be compostable a material must respect additional criteria. Some biodegradable products, while deserving the qualification, are made of components that, most of the time with additives present in their composition, will fragment, degrade, in nature.
An industrial composting must respect precise norms (temperature 75°-80°, humidity rate 65-70% and oxygen rate 18-20%). Under these conditions, the composting process takes about 12 weeks.
One of the numerous advantages of bagasse products is that they biodegrade in 90 days, as opposed to the 400 years that plastic takes to breakdown. Changing from plastic disposable dinnerware to bagasse disposable tableware is a positive step toward reducing our carbon footprint.
Sugarcane bagasse is fibrous, giving it a different texture than plastic counterparts. Although many products, such as plates and bowls, are smoothed out, bagasse dinnerware will feel slightly rough and akin to cardboard.
Bagasse containers and tableware are equally as sturdy and long-lasting as plastic containers and tableware. Both hot and cold food can be served on plates and containers.
