The Protocol limits 4G mobile phones’ transmission power to a maximum of 23dBm (0.2w). Although this power is not very large, the frequency of the 4G mainstream band (FDD 1800MHz) is relatively low, and the transmission loss is relatively small.
First of all, the mainstream frequency band of 5G is 3.5GHz, a high frequency, larger propagation path loss, poor penetration capability, weaker mobile phone capabilities, and low transmit power; therefore, the uplink is easy to become the system bottleneck. Second, 5G Is based on TDD mode, and the uplink and downlink are sent in time division.
Moreover, the deployment model of 5G is flexible, and the networking is complex.
3GPP has defined multiple power levels for the terminal.
Due to the high frequency and transmission characteristics is different from Sub 6G, the application scenarios are more considered in fixes access or non-mobile phone use.
As long as the mobile phone works in FDD mode, the maximum transmits power is only 23dBm, while in TDD mode, or non-independent networking, 4G and 5G are both TDD mode, the maximum transmit power can be relaxed to 26dBm.
As we all know, the telecommunication industry has always had different opinions on whether electromagnetic radiation. Still, for the sake of safety, the transmission power of mobile phones must be strictly limited.
These health standards all point to one indicator: SAR, which is specifically used to measure the effects of near field radiation from mobile phones and other portable communications devices. SAR is a specific Absorption Ratio.
It is defined as the power absorbed per mass of tissue and has watts units per kilogram (W/kg). China’s national standard draws on European standards and stipulates: “the average SAR value of any 10g of biological for any six minutes shall not exceed 2.0W/Kg.
