PIM is generated by nonlinear mixing between two or more carrier frequencies, and the resulting signal contains additional undesired frequencies or intermodulation products. As the word “passive” in the name “passive intermodulation” means the same, the above-mentioned nonlinear mixing that causes PIM does not involve active devices, but is usually made of metal materials and interconnected devices.
• At least one thin oxide layer exists on most metal surfaces, which can cause tunneling effects or, in a nutshell, lead to a reduction in conductive area. Some people think that this phenomenon can produce the Schottky effect.
When various signals are combined, PIM, which causes interference to the LTE signal, is generated. Antennas, duplexers, cables, dirty or loose connectors, and damaged RF equipment and metal objects located near or within a cellular base station may be sources of PIM.
