This has nothing to do with a 'pass' defined above. A pass move replaces an over-(under-)strand with another over-(under-)strand where both strands have the same ends.
In this diagram the new red strand has fewer passes than the old green strand. Therefore this diagram shows a P- move.
In this diagram the new red strand has one more pass than the old green strand, therefore this is a P+ move.
where one can flip a loop 4 times and instantly get the unknot are easy to spot by following the knot line and looking for an arc with both ends at the same crossing. The order of performing R1 moves does not matter.
This loop can also be removed when lying entirely underneath:
Similarly to R1 moves it is easy to spot prototype R2 moves like here where two R2 moves need to be done before an R1 move yields the unknot:
P- moves replace an over-strand with one having less over-passes or an under-strand with one having less under-passes. In both cases the number of crossings is reduced.
The more consecutive passes of one sort one finds, the higher the chance to find a different route that needs less passes.
The following example shows how R3 moves can still be useful by making P- moves possible. As described in the first section, an R3 hole is surrounded by a top arc with 2 over-pass ends (here A,B), a middle arc with 1 over-pass end (C) and 1 under-pass end (B) and a bottom arc with 2 under-pass ends (here A,C).
and is also reducing the number of crossings by 2. Both diagrams can be simplified further through P- and R1 moves resulting finally in the unknot.
Let us practise that with an example.
For each one we show in light blue the three arcs that are involved. What is easily overlooked is the third one where the hole is the whole outside space which is 'surrounded' by only 3 arcs.
